{"type":"frame-snapshot","snapshot":{"callId":"kluo@361","phase":"after","pageId":"page@08915c7b0e63e53b39c8c340cf55b74d","frameId":"frame@fa995c39053590f86190be84bdafd18c","frameUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:4173/","doctype":"html","html":["HTML",{"__playwright_scroll_top_":"1840","lang":"en"},[[372,9]],[[378,19]],["BODY",{},[[378,20]],["DIV",{"id":"root"},["DIV",{"class":"app-shell","data-reduced-motion":"true"},[[378,22]],[[378,50]],["MAIN",{"id":"main","class":"main-content "},["SECTION",{"class":"interior-screen"},[[372,11]],[[372,13]],[[375,11]],["DIV",{"class":"inspection","data-mission":"apollo-11"},[[370,1]],["SECTION",{"class":"quest-header","aria-label":"Apollo investigation"},["DIV",{"class":"quest-heading-row"},["DIV",{},[[77,1]],["H2",{"tabindex":"-1"},"Investigation complete"]],[[353,10]]],["OL",{"class":"quest-progress","aria-label":"Mission progress","style":"--quest-count: 5;"},[[279,3]],[[225,3]],[[151,3]],[[82,3]],["LI",{"data-state":"completed"},[[353,41]],["SPAN",{},[[353,42]],["SMALL",{},"completed"]]]],[[11,1]]],[[370,20]],["DIV",{"class":"inspection-layout"},["DIV",{"class":"inspection-stage"},["DIV",{"class":"spacecraft-viewport","data-model-state":"loaded","data-camera-mode":"ladder","data-model-meshes":"157","data-model-materials":"12","data-model-textures":"3","data-model-triangles":"97588","data-camera-position":"2.334,2.188,10.462"},[[215,15]],["DIV",{"style":"position: relative; width: 100%; height: 100%; overflow: hidden; pointer-events: auto; touch-action: none;"},["DIV",{"style":"width: 100%; height: 100%;"},["CANVAS",{"__playwright_bounding_rect__":"{\"left\":61,\"top\":-1046.09375,\"right\":1079,\"bottom\":-376.09375}","style":"display: block; width: 1018px; height: 670px; touch-action: auto;","data-engine":"three.js r180","width":"1018","height":"670"},[[366,1]]]]]],[[370,29]]],[[212,1]]],["SECTION",{"id":"quest-workbench","class":"mission-conclusion","aria-label":"Apollo mission conclusion"},["P",{"class":"eyebrow"},"5"," INVESTIGATIONS / ONE RECOVERED STORY"],["H2",{},"They came home. Part of Eagle stayed."],["P",{"class":"conclusion-lead"},"That was how it was meant to work."],["P",{},"The descent stage had landed them safely and given the ascent stage a place to leave from. Its journey ended at Tranquility Base. The crew's did not."],["P",{},"Armstrong and Aldrin carried samples back to Columbia. The experiments they deployed opened other ways to study the Moon: ground vibrations, and light returned to Earth."],["P",{},"Decades later, an orbiting camera could still pick out the landing stage. A machine no longer flying, still part of the record."],["OL",{"class":"conclusion-findings"},["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"The Machine in Two Halves"],["P",{},"The strange shape makes sense now: one stage for landing, another for leaving. The separation was a plan, not a failure."]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"The Landing Problem"],["P",{},"A controlled descent got Eagle down. The landing gear made touchdown a place the crew could work from."]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"The First Step"],["P",{},"Cabin, platform, ladder, footpad, soil. Even the first step depended on the shape of the machine that brought them there."]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"The Science That Stayed"],["P",{},"One listened to the ground. One returned light to Earth. Neither needed a seat on the journey home."]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"The Part That Stayed"],["P",{},"The descent stage stayed because the design worked. The crew could leave the landing hardware behind and carry their discoveries home."]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"completed-findings"},["SUMMARY",{},"Review the ","11"," recovered evidence records"],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-grid"},["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"A cabin that could leave",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{},"The upper stage carried the crew cabin and an engine of its own. After the surface stay, it took Armstrong and Aldrin back into lunar orbit to rendezvous with Columbia."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/history/apollos-lunar-module-bridged-technological-leap-to-the-moon/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Lunar Module engineering"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/history/apollo-11-mission-overview/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Apollo 11 mission overview"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: A cabin that could leave"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"A platform built to stay",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{},"The lower stage carried the landing engine, propellant and landing gear. It supported surface operations and served as the platform for liftoff. It did not have to accompany the crew back to orbit."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/history/apollos-lunar-module-bridged-technological-leap-to-the-moon/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Lunar Module engineering"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/history/apollo-11-mission-overview/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Apollo 11 mission overview"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: A platform built to stay"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"Slow down before touchdown",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{},"The throttleable descent engine supplied braking thrust during the landing approach. The crew could control the final descent and choose a safe touchdown area. This engine was separate from the ascent engine."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/history/apollos-lunar-module-bridged-technological-leap-to-the-moon/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Lunar Module engineering"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: Slow down before touchdown"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"Stay upright after touchdown",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{},"The landing gear was designed for energy absorption and stability. Four legs and their footpads supported the Lunar Module on the surface; they could not substitute for rocket thrust during descent."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19720018253","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Landing gear technical report"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Apollo_11.pdf","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Apollo 11 press kit (PDF)"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: Stay upright after touchdown"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"The route out of Eagle",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{},"The astronauts left through the forward hatch, crossed the small egress platform and climbed down the ladder on the forward landing leg. They reached its footpad before stepping onto the lunar soil."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Apollo_11.pdf","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Apollo 11 press kit (PDF)"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/ochmo-tb-022-apollo-lunar-lander.pdf#page=6","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Lunar lander human factors, p. 6 (PDF)"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: The route out of Eagle"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"The missing last rung",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{},"The ladder stopped above the footpad. NASA allowed clearance for collapsible landing gear and uncertainty about the surface. Getting from the lowest rung to the footpad required crossing that gap."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/ochmo-tb-022-apollo-lunar-lander.pdf#page=6","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Lunar lander human factors, p. 6 (PDF)"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: The missing last rung"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"PSEP: listening to the Moon",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{},"The Passive Seismic Experiment Package measured ground vibrations, including signals used to investigate lunar seismic activity. It was one of the two basic EASEP experiments. Its sensors did not measure Earth–Moon distance."],["FIGURE",{"class":"science-diagram"},["svg",{"viewBox":"0 0 360 105","role":"img","aria-label":"Schematic: lunar ground vibrations are sensed by a seismometer and recorded."},["path",{"d":"M12 75H348M20 57l10-8 10 16 10-27 10 38 10-26 10 7h35","fill":"none","stroke":"#a4ced3","stroke-width":"2"}],["rect",{"x":"130","y":"40","width":"55","height":"35","fill":"#29414a","stroke":"#a4ced3"}],["path",{"d":"M185 57H220l8-9 8 17 8-17 8 9h80","fill":"none","stroke":"#a4ced3","stroke-width":"2"}],["text",{"x":"15","y":"96"},"Ground motion"],["text",{"x":"123","y":"25"},"Sensor"],["text",{"x":"250","y":"96"},"Record"]],["FIGCAPTION",{},"Instrument schematic; not to scale."]],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-one-small-step-one-giant-leap/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Apollo 11 surface experiments"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Apollo_11.pdf","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Apollo 11 press kit (PDF)"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: PSEP: listening to the Moon"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"LRRR: a return address for light",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{},"The Laser Ranging Retroreflector returned laser pulses sent from Earth. Timing the round trip enabled precise measurements of Earth–Moon distance. The reflector did not generate the laser beam."],["FIGURE",{"class":"science-diagram"},["svg",{"viewBox":"0 0 360 105","role":"img","aria-label":"Schematic: a laser pulse travels from an Earth station to a lunar reflector and returns to Earth."},["circle",{"cx":"30","cy":"50","r":"18","fill":"#29414a","stroke":"#a4ced3"}],["rect",{"x":"312","y":"31","width":"25","height":"38","fill":"#29414a","stroke":"#a4ced3"}],["path",{"d":"M55 38H303l-9-5m9 5-9 5M305 64H57l9-5m-9 5 9 5","fill":"none","stroke":"#a4ced3","stroke-width":"2"}],["text",{"x":"111","y":"25"},"Laser pulse →"],["text",{"x":"106","y":"90"},"← Returned light"],["text",{"x":"4","y":"103"},"Earth"],["text",{"x":"290","y":"103"},"Moon"]],["FIGCAPTION",{},"Instrument schematic; not to scale."]],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-one-small-step-one-giant-leap/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Apollo 11 surface experiments"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Apollo_11.pdf","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Apollo 11 press kit (PDF)"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: LRRR: a return address for light"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"A small science package",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{},"Apollo 11 carried EASEP, the Early Apollo Scientific Experiments Package: PSEP and LRRR were its two basic experiments. Later missions carried the expanded ALSEP. A separate solar-wind composition experiment also flew on Apollo 11. There was no lunar rover on this mission."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Apollo_11.pdf","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Apollo 11 press kit (PDF)"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: A small science package"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"A rendezvous, not a direct flight home",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{},"After landing and surface exploration, Eagle’s ascent stage lifted off. It rendezvoused and docked with Columbia in lunar orbit. Armstrong, Aldrin and the samples transferred to Columbia; the ascent stage was later jettisoned. The crew returned to Earth in Columbia’s command module."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/history/apollo-11-mission-overview/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Apollo 11 mission overview"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: A rendezvous, not a direct flight home"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"One last look, decades later",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{},"Images from the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter Camera show the Apollo 11 descent stage and the trails left by the astronauts. The object left at the landing site was not the cabin that carried them back to orbit."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/apollo-11-landing-site/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Apollo 11 landing site, LROC"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: One last look, decades later"]]]]],["P",{"class":"conclusion-signoff"},"The missions ended. Their stories live on."],["P",{"class":"journey-storage"},"Revisit the spacecraft, review the evidence, or restart the investigation above."]],[[370,77]]]]],[[378,101]]]],[[378,104]]]],"viewport":{"width":1440,"height":900},"timestamp":78175.317,"wallTime":1794744063694,"collectionTime":373.7999999821186,"resourceOverrides":[],"isMainFrame":true}}
